  "id": "COMPOUND:41664 - PROTEIN:3897",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between Cytisine, N-formyl- and Cholesteryl ester transfer protein (CETP), through a series of target interactions involving the enzyme 8-O-(P-Coumaroyl)-1(10)E,4(5)E-Humuladien-8-Ol. This pathway is supported by multiple evidence scores, with Cytisine, N-formyl- being identified as a target of 8-O-(P-Coumaroyl)-1(10)E,4(5)E-Humuladien-8-Ol, which in turn targets CETP. The presence of multiple overlapping pathways, including one involving Myosin light chain kinase 3 and another involving ATP-binding cassette sub-family A member 1, indicates a broader network of interactions that may contribute to the regulation of CETP. The evidence suggests that Cytisine, N-formyl- may influence CETP through the activation of these downstream targets, potentially modulating CETP activity. However, the exact mechanism by which Cytisine, N-formyl- exerts its effect on CETP remains unclear, and further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct relationship.",
